Generated by [hexa-go](https://github.com/erwinhermantodev/hexa-go) using hexagonal architecture
(ports and adapters): HTTP handlers call services, services call repositories, repositories talk to the database.

## Quick start

```bash
go mod tidy                 # resolve dependencies
docker-compose up -d postgres
go run main.go              # serves on :8080
```

Check it is up: `curl localhost:8080/api/v1/health`

Configuration lives in `configs/config.yaml` and can be overridden with environment variables (see below).
`.env.example` lists the variables but is **not loaded automatically**; export them in your shell or use
`docker-compose`.

> The generated code does not create database tables. Add `db.AutoMigrate(...)` in `main.go` for your models, or
> manage the schema with [golang-migrate](https://github.com/golang-migrate/migrate) (`make migrate-up`).

## Project layout

```
main.go                          wiring: config, database, repositories, services, handlers, server
configs/config.yaml              default configuration
locales/                         message catalogs (en, id) read by utils.LoadMessages
model/                           entities plus request/response types
repository/                      data access (GORM), one interface per model
service/                         business logic
transport/http/handler/          Echo handlers
transport/http/routes/routes.go  route table, including /swagger and /api/v1/health
transport/grpc/                  gRPC server skeleton (not started by main.go yet)
utils/                           config, database, JWT, password, validator, response helpers
internal/modules/                co-located modules created with `hexa-go add module`
migrations/                      SQL migrations for golang-migrate
```

`main.go` and `routes.go` contain `// [MARKER]` comments. `hexa-go add ...` inserts code above them, so leave
them in place.

## Adding code

Install the CLI (`go install github.com/erwinhermantodev/hexa-go@latest`), then from this directory:

```bash
hexa-go add model Product -f "Name:string::required" -f "Price:float64::required,gt=0"
hexa-go add service Payment
hexa-go add handler Report
hexa-go add module Blog -f "Title:string::required"
```

`add model` creates the model, repository, service, handler and tests, and registers them in `main.go` and
`routes.go`. Field syntax is `Name:Type[:GormOptions[:Validation]]`; see the hexa-go documentation.

## REST endpoints

Every model with a handler gets these routes under `/api/v1` (the path is the kebab-case plural of the model name,
for example `OrderItem` becomes `/order-items`):

| Method | Path | Handler |
| --- | --- | --- |
| POST | `/{models}` | create |
| GET | `/{models}` | list |
| GET | `/{models}/:id` | get one |
| PUT | `/{models}/:id` | update |
| DELETE | `/{models}/:id` | delete |

Responses use one envelope: `{"status": 200, "message": "...", "data": ...}`.

## Configuration

Values come from `configs/config.yaml`, then environment variables. Every key can be set as `APP_<KEY>`
(for example `APP_DATABASE_DRIVER=sqlite`). These shorter names are also accepted:

| Variable | Config key | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DB_HOST` / `DB_PORT` / `DB_USER` / `DB_PASSWORD` / `DB_NAME` | `database_*` | `localhost` / `5432` / `postgres` / `postgres` / `{{.Name}}` |
| `APP_DATABASE_DRIVER` | `database_driver` | `postgres` (also `mysql`, `sqlite`) |
| `JWT_SECRET` | `jwt_secret` | `your-secret-key` (change it) |
| `SERVER_PORT` | `server_port` | `8080` |
| `GRPC_PORT` | `grpc_port` | `9090` |

## Commands

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `make run` / `make build` | run or build the server |
| `make test` | run all tests (the repository tests use in-memory SQLite and need cgo) |
| `make docker-up` / `make docker-down` | start or stop the app and PostgreSQL |
| `make swag-init` | generate Swagger docs (needs `go install github.com/swaggo/swag/cmd/swag@latest`); UI at `/swagger/index.html` |
| `make migrate-up` / `make migrate-down` | apply or revert migrations (needs the `migrate` CLI) |

## What is included

- Echo HTTP server with request logging, panic recovery, CORS and graceful shutdown (10 s on SIGINT/SIGTERM)
- GORM with PostgreSQL, MySQL or SQLite
- Validation with go-playground/validator on create requests
- JWT and bcrypt helpers in `utils/` (no auth endpoints are generated yet)
- Dockerfile, docker-compose with PostgreSQL, GitHub Actions workflow

